From: godotjs
Subject: [godotjs] libffi: put win64_armasm.obj on the link of everything that links libffi

Windows/arm64 builds libffi's assembly by assembling src/aarch64/win64_armasm.S into
a standalone object and naming that object in the libffi target's own `libraries`.
libffi is a static library, so nothing ever links it and the entry is never used: the
three symbols win64_armasm.S exports - ffi_call_SYSV, ffi_closure_SYSV,
ffi_closure_SYSV_V, all called from src/aarch64/ffi.c - stay undefined, and the
first binary to link libffi.lib stops at its link step (node_mksnapshot.exe:
error LNK2019 three times, then LNK1120). Every other platform gets those symbols
inside the archive, because there sysv.S is one of libffi's sources.

Repeat the entry under link_settings, which gyp merges into the link line of every
dependent - the same route deps/crates/crates.gyp already uses to reach those
binaries. Windows/arm64 only, and the object is still named where it was, so nothing
that saw it before stops seeing it.
diff --git a/deps/libffi/libffi.gyp b/deps/libffi/libffi.gyp
index f3122a4..a670268 100644
--- a/deps/libffi/libffi.gyp
+++ b/deps/libffi/libffi.gyp
@@ -313,6 +313,15 @@
           'libraries': [
             '<(INTERMEDIATE_DIR)/win64_armasm.obj',
           ],
+          # The block above only reaches a link of libffi itself, and a static
+          # library is never linked, so dependents never saw the object and stopped
+          # with unresolved ffi_call_SYSV / ffi_closure_SYSV / ffi_closure_SYSV_V.
+          # link_settings is the block gyp hands to the dependents' link lines.
+          'link_settings': {
+            'libraries': [
+              '<(INTERMEDIATE_DIR)/win64_armasm.obj',
+            ],
+          },
           'actions': [
             {
               'action_name': 'assemble_win64_arm_asm',
